{
  "dom_id": "step-q31676",
  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "The velocity vector $v=\u27e8u,v\u27e9$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nv=\u27e82{t}^{2}-4t,5t-{t}^{3}\u27e9.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t=2.$",
  "worked_solution": "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}-4t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5t-{t}^{3})\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e84t-4,\\,5-3{t}^{2}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the acceleration at time $t=2,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(2) & =\u27e84(2)-4,\\,5-3(2)^{2}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e88-4,\\,5-12\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e84,\\,-7\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$",
